  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Gallery of Four Rooms",
    "overview": "devayu is one gallery shell and four whole rooms. The shell is a warm night wall with four lit canvases on it and almost no chrome; its job is to stay out of the way. Each room is a complete, separate visual world built from its movement's practice (type, materials, composition, motion), and each one rebuilds the same true story from the same data in `src/data/`. Nothing is a palette swap over a shared template: rooms do not share faces, colors, buttons or easing. What they share is structure: a world mount that marks `html[data-world]` and binds Esc to the gallery, cover transitions made of the destination's material, one sound-control contract, one content model, and one craft floor.\n\nDensity runs high inside the rooms (posters, newspapers, shelves, still lifes) and low on the wall. Every room's hero is the movement's scene only, with no name; \"I'm Ayush\" begins below it. Every room ends at a way to reach him. The rejected modes, confirmed by the direction contract, are the one-page-with-a-theme-swap default and movement pastiche (primary confetti, comic POW bubbles, grunge collage, low-poly).",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One shell, four sovereign worlds; each room's tokens are scoped to its root class and never leak.",
      "Every composition is original SVG/CSS from the movement's grammar; no reproduced artworks, photos or brands.",
      "Motion is the movement's own mechanism: Bauhaus cams on rails, Dada stepped chance, Pop mechanical print pulls, Cubist viewpoint shifts.",
      "Sound is a design material: four generative themes on one 104 bpm transport, crossfading on the beat between rooms.",
      "Phones are first-class: the wall becomes four stacked bands, every room has a dedicated phone pass."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Sealed Room Rule",
        "body": "A room's colours are declared on its root (`.bh-room`, `.dd-root`, `.pp-room`, `.cb-room`) and on `html[data-world=…]`; nothing room-specific may live in `globals.css`. A new surface in a room uses that room's tokens only.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Earned Colour Rule",
        "body": "Cubism starts analytic (ochre, umber, greys on canvas) and only reaches full Synthetic colour as the visitor scrolls (the ground is mixed by scroll progress). Saturated Cubist colour does not appear in the hero.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Vermilion Rule",
        "body": "In Bauhaus and Dada, vermilion is the only hot colour carrying structure; Bauhaus yellow and ultramarine stay on the stage and the kit.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lowercase Rule",
        "body": "Everything in the Bauhaus room is lowercase, set by the room root; never reintroduce capitals there.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mixed Printing Rule",
        "body": "Dada headings are built word by word from different faces (and ransom letters on chance), never one face for a whole heading.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Own-Face Label Rule",
        "body": "On the gallery wall, each canvas names itself in its room's display face; the wall's own chrome is Archivo only.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Material Depth Rule",
        "body": "Depth is always a material behaviour of the room (wall light, paper lift, print register, passage), never a generic card shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Register Rule",
        "body": "Pop's black key lands off-register by (+4px, +3px) by default; this hard offset is Pop's print grammar and belongs to Pop alone.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** scope every new rule to its room root or `html[data-world]`; CSS stays loaded after client-side moves between rooms.",
      "**Do** build each room's micro-interactions from its movement's mechanism: rails and hard-stop cams (cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.9, 0.1, 1)) in Bauhaus, stepped chance (steps(2–3)) in Dada, mechanical steps (steps(2, jump-none), 90ms) in Pop, hard 150ms light steps and feathering passage in Cubism.",
      "**Do** keep heroes as the scene only; start the name and introduction below.",
      "**Do** give every room a dedicated phone pass and keep touch targets at 44px or more.",
      "**Do** provide a reduced-motion state where every print, cut and plane is already in place.",
      "**Do** draw icons and arrows as authored SVG in the room's own stroke (Cubism arrows: one 1.75 stroke, square caps, open head)."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** let one room's palette, face or easing appear in another room; the gallery is the only place all four meet, each inside its own canvas.",
      "**Don't** reproduce real artworks, artists' images or real brands; compose from the movement's grammar.",
      "**Don't** fall into movement pastiche: primary confetti, comic POW bubbles, grunge collage, low-poly.",
      "**Don't** use Unicode glyphs or icon fonts as icons.",
      "**Don't** add capitals to the Bauhaus room or a single uniform face to a Dada heading.",
      "**Don't** put saturated Synthetic colour at the top of the Cubism room."
    ]
  }
